# Rule: Update planton.ai Copy (from feedback to a draft and a handoff)

**Purpose**: Turn feedback, a product change, or a positioning change into a reviewed draft of the words a page will say, with every sentence beside what backs it, and a handoff that names the data files the words go into. The companion rule, `implement-planton-ai-copy-writing.mdc`, takes the handoff to a built, captured, reviewed change. One session may do both; the two documents are still written, because they are the record.

## What the site is

The site is data first. Every sentence that states what Planton is or does lives in `src/data/` and names the chapter of the story it mirrors; pages render records and state nothing of their own. The story is `company/marketing/positioning/the-planton-story.md` in the company repository, mirrored as `src/data/story.ts`; the page records are `trust.ts`, `product.ts`, `distributions.ts`, `personas.ts`, `compare.ts`, `desktop.ts`; the vocabulary is `positioning.ts` (the umbrella never wears an analogy; each hub has exactly one); doors are `doors.ts`; prices are `pricing.ts`; counts are `platform-stats.ts`; quotes are `testimonials.ts`. Read `README.md` at the site root before the first draft; it names every law the words obey.

A copy change is therefore one of three things: a sentence in a record changes; a chapter of the story changes (and the record follows); or a new record is written for a new page. Never a sentence typed into a component.

## Before drafting

1. Put the materials in `content/copywriting/_workspace/` (gitignored). PDFs become Markdown with `python3 content/copywriting/_rules/pdf_converter/convert_pdf.py`.
2. Read the story end to end, then the record of every page the change touches, then the pages themselves in the built export or a capture.
3. Name the visitor the page is for (a developer with a coding agent open, landing from a thread; the engineering leader that developer forwarded the link to; the person deciding whether to install tonight) and the one question they open the page to answer.

## The draft

Create `content/copywriting/_stage-area/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>/draft-1.md`. For every sentence the page will say, write the sentence and, beside it, what backs it: the chapter and proof index (`ch 3, proof 0`), the positioning key, the pricing or counts constant, or the public document (`public/docs/...`) or knowledge article it restates. A sentence with nothing beside it is a new claim, and a new claim is a story change first (below) or it is cut.

Conventions: titles, labels, and buttons in Title Case; everything read as prose in sentence case. Numbers carry their unit and their source, and appear only through `platform-stats.ts` or `pricing.ts`. No dollar-savings figure. No component called compliant. Competitors described, never named. Nothing from the story's last chapter (what is next) on a page. Doors by key from `doors.ts`, never a new button label.

There is no HTML preview. The built page under the capture harness (`make capture`) is the preview, and the independent reviewer grades pixels.

## The claim check and the two cold reads

Read the draft against its sources sentence by sentence and record the result in the draft under a heading of its own: which sentences held, which were reworded to the source's words, which were cut. Then read the whole draft twice, cold: once as the named visitor, once as a senior copywriter for developer-tools sales pages that convert (first-screen value and who it is for, one claim per screen with proof beside it, the objection answered where it arises, no jargon or metaphor in a heading, the same door in the same words). Write what each read changed.

## When the draft finds the story wrong

If a sentence the page needs contradicts the story, or the story overclaims where the page would expose it, the story changes first: archive it under `company/marketing/positioning/archive/` with a timestamp, edit the chapter, bump the version line with one sentence saying why, mirror `story.ts`, and check the knowledge mirrors named in the story's header. The page never papers over the story.

## The handoff

Write `handoff.md` in the same folder: the routes; where the copy is (which record, which chapter each sentence names); the argument in one paragraph; the component mapping (which template renders the record, which primitives it composes, anything lifted to `components/marketing/`); the revisions the cold reads made; what the page must not claim; how it will be verified. The handoff is the record a future session reads to understand why the page says what it says.

## Then

Follow `implement-planton-ai-copy-writing.mdc`.
